messages:
Devin: enterprise customer on dedicated plan reports TS SDK failing when using JSON streaming mode. error seen in production: \"Unexpected token } in JSON at position 0\" after ~6-12s of streaming. they attached a repro and logs.

Maria: thanks — can you paste the minimal repro and the request id? also which SDK version and node version?

Devin: request id x-rd-req=rdp_09G, SDK @redwood/sdk v1.5.1, Node 20.4.0. minimal handler from customer: 

```
const stream = client.responses.stream({ model: 'redwood-ts-1', input: prompt, json: true });
let buf = '';
stream.on('data', chunk => {
  buf += chunk.toString('utf8');
  try {
    const obj = JSON.parse(buf);
    handle(obj);
    buf = '';
  } catch (e) {
    // expect more chunks, keep buffering
  }
});
```

They report that most messages parse fine, but intermittently an array in the structured output is split across chunks such that intermediate buffer ends with trailing commas or partial strings (e.g., chunk ends with `...,` and next chunk starts `]}`), causing parse errors or duplicated punctuation after naive concat.

Ajay: that matches a chunk-boundary problem. The server streams tokens, not NDJSON frames; asking for `json: true` returns a logical JSON document but tokenized streaming can split inside arrays/strings. TS code doing naive JSON.parse on every data event is fragile. We should recommend NDJSON or provide a helper.

Devin: customer insists it was previously more tolerant in their python client; they're blocked in prod. they want an official workaround or a patch.

Patrick: short term guidance — advise customers to use server-side line-delimited JSON if available, or client-side buffering until a boundary heuristic indicates completion. medium term — add a jsonStream helper to the TS SDK that buffers and emits only complete JSON objects. long term — server should support an explicit structured streaming format (NDJSON or event frames) for all SDKs.

SDK-QA: quick repro created locally: simulated a 8k array where a single item gets split across TCP chunks. example chunks captured: 

```
chunkA: "{\"items\":[\"first\",\"midd"
chunkB: "le-split-inside-item-very-long\",\"last\"]}"
```

Naive JSON.parse(buf) fails on chunkA; it only succeeds after chunkB arrives. Heuristics that wait for closing brace can work but are brittle if multiple JSON objects are streamed back-to-back.

Maria: customer asks for an immediate shim they can drop into their app. any concise snippet we can share that is safer than naive parse?

Ajay: suggestion — simple bracket balance check + minimal NDJSON fallback. paste helper: 

```
function tryParseBufferedJSON(buffer) {
  // quick depth balancer for { } and [ ] — ignore strings for simplicity (best-effort)
  let depth = 0; let inStr = false; let esc = false;
  for (let i = 0; i < buffer.length; i++) {
    const ch = buffer[i];
    if (esc) { esc = false; continue; }
    if (ch === '\\') { esc = true; continue; }
    if (ch === '\"') { inStr = !inStr; continue; }
    if (inStr) continue;
    if (ch === '{' || ch === '[') depth++;
    if (ch === '}' || ch === ']') depth--;
  }
  if (depth === 0) return JSON.parse(buffer);
  return null;
}

// usage: append chunks to buf and call tryParseBufferedJSON(buf) on each data event; only consume when it returns object.
```

Devin: sent shim to customer with caution that this is a stopgap. they asked if it's an SDK bug — they expect SDK to not surface partial JSON to user code.

Patrick: action items: 1) KB article explaining streaming JSON vs NDJSON and recommended client patterns (short-term), 2) quick patch for TS SDK: add a `responses.jsonStream()` wrapper that internally buffers and emits validated JSON objects (1-2 day ETA), 3) server-side task to support explicit NDJSON/event frames (longer).

SDK-QA: opening a unit test in ts-sdk: tests/streaming-json-boundary.spec.ts to reproduce and assert our jsonStream() returns intact objects regardless of chunk boundaries. I'll link PR once green.

Maria: created escalation INC-2026-973 and notified account team; assigned to Ajay + SDK-QA. SLA initial response 24h. @Devin pls attach full logs and the customer's session transcript to the ticket.

Devin: uploaded logs and sample session to the incident. will keep customer updated with the shim + ETA for SDK patch.

Support-bot: incident INC-2026-973 assigned to @Ajay, @SDK-QA.

Ajay: I'll iterate on a minimal jsonStream() prototype and post a PR today. will include tests and a migration note in the SDK changelog.
